  "metadata": {
    "title": "Social Immunology and Information Oncology: Transdisciplinary Models of Network Dynamics for Biomedical Investigation",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.20279008",
    "publication_date": "2026-05-19",
    "description": "<p>The growing complexity of biological and social systems demands modelling<br>paradigms that transcend disciplinary boundaries. This work explores a<br>profound conceptual and methodological analogy between the dynamics of<br>artificial intelligence (AI) in human societies and the pathogenesis of complex<br>diseases such as cancer and autoimmune disorders. We argue that computational models developed to understand the spread of (dis)information, the&nbsp;formation of echo chambers, and adversarial information warfare offer robust<br>frameworks for generating new hypotheses in biomedical investigation.</p>",
